nir: store the algebraic search automaton's tables compactly (GFOpenGPU,
10 November 2026). On the 68k they were 40% of GL.module:
nir_opt_algebraic.c.o was 7,765,896 bytes, of which bcsel's transition
table alone (137^3 uint16_t entries) was 5,142,708, and the 161
per-opcode filters (5,100 uint16_t each) another 1.6 MB.

The automaton is built exactly as before; only how its tables are stored
changes. nir_algebraic.py (CompactTables) emits:
- each distinct filter and table once, by name;
- a filter whose values fit a byte (all of them, in Mesa 26.2.4) paged:
  the number of a 32-byte block for each 32 states, and each distinct
  block once for the whole pass (<pass>_filter_pages);
- for an opcode with two or more sources, when it is smaller, a "row
  table": n^(k-1) row numbers for the first k-1 sources' filtered states,
  then each distinct row of n entries once (bcsel: 781 distinct rows of
  18,769, 251,532 bytes in place of 5,142,708);
- each distinct per-state transform list once, and struct transform's
  condition_offset as a uint16_t (UINT16_MAX ends a list, in place of ~0).

nir_search.h/.c look these up in place, with one more read for a paged
filter or a row table. Nothing is expanded at run time: no memory and no
time at first use. struct per_op_table is 16 bytes (was 12).
nir_opt_algebraic.c.o: 7,765,896 -> 1,036,648 bytes. The generated .c is
6.5 MB (was 52 MB) and is generated and compiled much faster.

Same shaders: as it emits the tables, the generator checks, for every
opcode, that the lookup on what it emits gives the original dense table's
next state for every combination of the sources' filtered states, the
filter's value for every state, and every state's original transform list
in the same order. library/modules/gl/tests/nir_tables_check.py checks the
emitted C again, against the C the unpatched generator makes.

